<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johann Strauss II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1899">1899</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Johann Strauss Jr., Johann Strauss, The Waltz King</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johann Strauss II was an Austrian composer of light music and a violinist, born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, the son of the composer Johann Strauss I, who wrote over 500 waltzes, polkas, quadrilles and marches, several operettas and a ballet. Known in his lifetime as The Waltz King, he did much to popularise the waltz in the 19th century. He made his debut, against his father's wishes, at Dommayer's Casino in Hietzing in October 1844. His best-known works include The Blue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the Emperor Waltz and Tales from the Vienna Woods, and the operettas Die Fledermaus and Der Zigeunerbaron. He died in Vienna in 1899 while composing the ballet Aschenbrödel.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-strauss-ii.jpg" alt="Johann Strauss II" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fritz Luckhardt / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Strauss_II_by_Fritz_Luckhardt_3-4_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
